en:
So you're into sports too, huh?
I've dabbled in all kinds of stuff myself.
I'm pretty good, if I say so myself.{p}

Tadokoro was overjoyed.
It felt like he'd found a true friend.
It wouldn't be going too far to call him a mentor for life.{p}

Even on Sundays.
As those days went on, Tadokoro gradually started getting frustrated.
Just talking wasn't enough for him anymore.{p}

So you do, huh.
Then let me tell you something interesting.
The one who took the photos of Tadokoro getting beaten up by the track team... I hear the name was {a}.{p}

Could just be a coincidence, but that sounds a lot like you, doesn't it?
Maybe your brother was...
Next time, try asking your brother.{p}

I see.
But there must be times you think you'd like to, right?
Listen, {a}.{p}

During that match, a player from the other school got hurt.
One of our team members accidentally jabbed a finger into that player's eye during the game.
Well, it only scratched the surface of the eye, so it didn't turn into anything serious.{p}

The boys' volleyball team decided to throw the cursed ball out again.
They wrapped the ball in cloth, tied it up tight with string, and threw it into the incinerator.
And then... the cursed ball came flying out of the blazing flames.{p}

Tadokoro went to complain to the club's advisor.
But that didn't change anything.
He was told the captain business had been decided by the members, so if he didn't like it, he should talk it over with all of them.{p}

Tadokoro started harassing whoever had become the new captain.
Tripping the new captain during practice, deliberately passing on club notices wrong and then blaming the captain for it...
All kinds of nasty, underhanded tricks.{p}

And that club was no good at all.
Calling it club activities, he harassed athletes from the sports clubs who were in the spotlight.
Letting the air out of soccer balls, cutting the strings of tennis rackets, putting thumbtacks in spiked shoes...{p}

It was the next morning that Tadokoro was found in the gym, dead, hanging by the neck.
A basketball team member who'd come in for morning practice found him.
They say next to him was a suicide note, filled line after line with sarcasm and spite.{p}

Sure, that kind of stuff was written in it too.
But what moved Tadokoro was something else written there.
That notebook had all sorts of things in it: passion for basketball, effective ways to practice, even what to eat.{p}

You think so?
Well, whatever, I'll keep going.
Tadokoro wished from the bottom of his heart that he could meet the senior.{p}

...Can't even imagine, huh?
Then I'll tell you.
Listen up, and listen good.{p}

Tadokoro kept his eyes shut for a while.
But whoever had appeared tapped him on the shoulder, and he opened his eyes.
And then...{p}

The police came and searched for these new members of his, but they couldn't find anyone who fit.
In the end, the whole thing was written off as Tadokoro's delusion.
And the cause of death was put down as sudden death, brought on by the hard practice he'd been doing every night.{p}

Oh.
Everyone is outside the entrance.
Are they scared, I wonder?{p}

Everyone was watching us from outside the entrance.
They couldn't have turned on the lights from over there.
Then who turned them on?{p}

"Eeeek!"
"Calm down, {a}☆"
Shindo grabbed my arm.{p}

We came all this way and you're calling it quits?
What a letdown.
That was your chance at a scoop.{p}
